# =============================================================================
# Script-level Receipt template (machine-readable JSONL: one JSON object per line)
# Concept owner: "kernel/K12 Quality Assurance/07 Audit Evidence Reuse and Invalidation.md"
#   -- This file is the lightweight receipt schema produced by deterministic
#      check scripts; the full K12/07 AuditReceipt (with scope /
#      acceptance_predicate / the three fingerprint kinds etc.) is completed
#      by the AuditPlan layer when entering the Audit Receipt Register; a
#      script receipt's receipt_id can be referenced directly as
#      evidence_ref / gate_receipts.
# Note: real receipts files are pure JSONL and contain no # comment lines;
#      the # lines in this template exist for documentation only. Field meanings:
#   receipt_id     : "audit-<tool>-<UTC timestamp>-<per-invocation random token>-<sequence>", append-only protocol ID, never reused (K12/07: receipt_id: audit-<stable-id>)
#   check          : check item identifier, e.g. "link-missing" / "vocab-unknown-value" / "proof-zero-field"
#   target         : checked object (vault-relative path[:line] or field name)
#   result         : pass / fail / candidate (candidate = produces candidates only, requires human judgement; see K10/04 Automated Language Review Boundary)
#   details        : detail message stating the concrete evidence; must not just say "QA passed" (K12/07)
#   checked_at     : UTC ISO8601 check time
#   tool           : producing script name (counterpart of K12/07 verifier.name)
#   tool_version   : script version (counterpart of K12/07 verifier.version; incompatible parser/verifier changes trigger Direct Invalidation)
#   gate_id        : stable K00/12 Gate ID emitted by registered deterministic
#                    gate producers; historical producer versions may lack it
#                    and remain history-only, never current gate input
#   invalidated_by : invalidation source (successor receipt_id or invalidation event description); null while valid (K12/07 Invalidation)
# Receipt JSONL is append-only. A writer never restores an older register image
# over concurrent appends; a partial or otherwise uncertain append preserves
# its runtime writer lock for restart reconciliation.
# Trust boundary: tool/actor/reviewer fields are declared labels, SHA-256 binds
# bytes but is not a signature, and append-only/immutable describe the supported
# protocol. A party controlling the repository, tools, and all evidence can
# fabricate an internally consistent history; stronger provenance needs an
# external signature, protected runner, or attestation service.
# Optional tool-specific fields may extend this lightweight layer. In
# `check_residual_content.py` receipts:
#   scan_id            : stable ID from the selected profile's Registered Scan Registry
#   config_fingerprint : `sha256:<hex>` over the exact profile-owned config bytes; null when the config could not be read or validated
# Both the explicit `--positive-controls-only` invocation and the following
# production invocation of a registered K12/09 item-6 verifier finish with a
# reliable summary carrying:
#   positive_control_result      : `passed`
#   positive_control_mode        : `production-classifier`
#   positive_control_count       : positive integer number of executed controls
#   positive_control_fingerprint : `sha256:<64 lowercase hex>` over the exact
#                                  verifier-owned control set
# These shared evidence fields do not prescribe the verifier's control-input
# representation. `check_batch_close.py` 1.3.0 requires the two final pass
# summaries to agree exactly on tool, tool_version, check, scan_id,
# config_fingerprint, and every positive_control_* field.
# `check_queue.py` pass receipts:
#   task_id                       : frozen task identity
#   queue_revision                : structural Queue revision
#   queue_state_revision          : lifecycle/hold revision
#   required_queue_sha256         : SHA-256 over the exact Queue bytes checked
#   remaining_required_work_units : non-terminal Required work-unit count
#   queue_check_mode              : consistency, require-ready:<id>,
#                                   require-revalidation:<batch-id>,
#                                   require-complete,
#                                   require-maintenance-complete, or resume-status
#                                   (the complete set, bound to checker version;
#                                   `check_queue.py` argparse is its owner)
# A successful `consistency` receipt also carries
# `repository_snapshot_sha256`: a path-sensitive SHA-256 over every regular
# repository file except the root `.git/` and `.cambium/` namespaces. The
# batch-close aggregator, its seven Closed List members, and its distinct
# global-review receipt bind that same value as `merged_snapshot_sha256`.
# `update_queue.py` recomputes it immediately before close; matching receipt
# strings without matching current repository bytes cannot authorize close.
# `check_corpus_plan.py` pass receipts carry
# `gate_id: corpus-plan-structure` and bind the exact selected Profile
# manifest, Profile Scope, Corpus Planning slot, Global Map, Capability Matrix,
# and Gap Register paths and SHA-256 values; task identity; Queue revisions; all three
# canonical state SHA-256 values; applicability; and the repository snapshot.
# Any bound byte or runtime revision change makes the receipt stale. A batch-
# close aggregator explicitly records `corpus_plan_required`, the sorted
# trigger set (`R13` and/or `manifest`), and either a distinct current
# `corpus_plan_receipt` or null. Manifest applicability is exact intersection
# with the selected Profile manifest, Scope, slot, artifacts, and validator-parsed Global Map entry,
# Matrix canonical/evidence, and Gap promoted/evidence paths; it never follows
# prose or inferred semantic relationships.
# `record_corpus_acceptance.py` appends a fresh structural receipt followed by
# one distinct `gate_id: corpus-plan-semantic-acceptance` receipt. The latter
# carries acceptance_id, acceptance_plan_path/SHA-256, authority_role_id,
# actor_role_id, decision_scope_id, structural_check_receipt, and the exact
# ordered capability_decisions. It repeats the structural byte/runtime binding
# so a Profile, artifact, canonical-state, Queue-revision, plan, authority, or
# repository-snapshot change makes the semantic acceptance stale. The decision
# plan lives only under `.cambium/deltas/corpus-plan-acceptances/`; no Markdown
# report is a receipt input or output.
# Examples of current stable-Gate producer protocols -- not the complete set;
# the Stable Gate ID Registry in K00/12 is the only complete enumeration of
# registered Gate IDs and their producers: check_links 1.5.0 /
# wiki-link-integrity; check_vocab 1.4.0 / frontmatter-vocabulary;
# check_residual_content 1.2.0 / registered-residual-content;
# check_batch_close 1.3.0 / batch-close; check_proof 1.15.0 /
# terminal-proof; and adopt_standards 1.2.0 / standards-adoption. Deterministic
# producers not shown above -- among them check_queue, check_corpus_plan, and
# record_corpus_acceptance -- follow the same rule. Every receipt
# created by one of these producer versions carries that exact Gate ID. The
# Stable Gate ID Registry's Tool/Tool-version/Check/Mode tuple selects the
# canonical summary or aggregator from the producer's diagnostic/member
# receipts and fails closed on a version mismatch.
# Every registered Gate producer additionally binds the current Required Queue
# identity -- `task_id`, `standards_version`, `selected_profile_manifest` --
# read from `.cambium/state/required_queue.yaml`, because a Standards
# revalidation boundary consumes a Gate receipt only when those three equal the
# live Queue values. `adopt_standards.py` binds the planned post-adoption
# identity, since its receipts are built before the state write and consumed
# after it. A run outside any Cambium runtime (no `.cambium/state/`) omits the
# three fields rather than writing null: an omitted field claims nothing, while
# an explicit null would satisfy consumers that only test field presence.
# `open -> merge-ready` additionally consumes one current in-batch wrapper:
#   tool                    : manual-attestation
#   tool_version            : 1.0.0
#   gate_id                 : batch-review
#   check                   : batch_gate
#   target / batch_id       : exact Queue batch ID
#   task_id                 : current Task ID
#   delta_page_receipt_ids  : sorted unique union of every Delta page's
#                             gate_receipts
# Page receipts may be valid historical evidence. They never authorize the
# lifecycle edge without this current wrapper, and invalidated IDs are rejected.
# `check_batch_close.py` is the sole close-bundle producer. Its successful
# publication appends, in order, seven distinct `closed_list_<member>` pass
# receipts, one `batch_global_review_attestation`, one distinct
# `batch_global_review`, the canonical `check_queue` consistency receipt, and
# one `batch_close_gate` aggregator. Every record binds task_id, batch_id, the
# same merged_snapshot_sha256, and producer version as applicable. Member and
# aggregator records bind declared integrator_id/reviewer_id labels; those
# labels must be nonempty and different. The attestation additionally carries a substantive
# details statement plus:
#   accepted_candidate_ids   : exact stable candidate IDs accepted in this run
#   accepted_candidate_types : exact tool:check types represented by the set
#   candidate_dispositions   : the exact ID/type/member/target/detail set and
#                              whether ID or type authorized each disposition
# The global review binds reviewer_attestation_receipt and the exact seven-
# member evidence mapping. The aggregator binds both review receipts, all
# three current state SHAs/revisions, Delta SHA/application receipt, Queue
# consistency receipt, unchanged before/after state SHAs, the registered
# gate_id=batch-close, explicit Work Spec binding, and Corpus Planning
# applicability/child binding. Only the current producer contract can
# authorize a close. Historical JSONL remains immutable but is not interpreted
# as current authorization. A failed run
# appends only a failed batch_close_gate attempt; an uncertain append preserves
# the runtime writer lock for restart reconciliation.
# A `require-maintenance-complete` pass additionally binds
# completion_semantics=maintenance, current Coverage/Progress/Queue bytes,
# every terminal batch id, applicable batch/close gate ids, and three current
# evidence receipts: maintenance_budget_manifest,
# maintenance_ledger_advanced, and maintenance_watermark_advanced. The first
# binds a closed schema-v2 manifest plus its complete candidate-state SHA,
# selected/deferred candidate IDs, and prior-run receipt; Coverage carries the
# same candidate records, and Queue carries exactly the selected object set.
# The latter two receipts bind distinct before/current after fingerprints. A
# restart may consume the latest current-compatible gate, but never a stale
# one. The pass receipt retains the candidate-state projection needed to check
# consecutive deferral age in the next maintenance run and the Task Contract
# fingerprint consumed by its canonical `update_task` completion.
# The watermark receipt binds both `watermark_run_id` (the enclosing
# maintenance run) and `watermark_batch_id` (the Queue batch that performed the
# final advance). These are intentionally distinct identities.
# `check_proof.py` terminal pass receipts additionally carry:
#   terminal_proof_path / terminal_proof_sha256
#   coverage_ledger_sha256 / progress_ledger_sha256 / required_queue_sha256
#   task/scope/contract/Standards/profile identity, Queue revisions/path,
#   remaining_required_work_units, queue_check_receipt, and the current
#   corpus_plan_check_receipt consumed from the Audit Receipt Register
# Queue transition receipts additionally carry before/after state, hold,
# state revision, Queue fingerprint, task id, Queue revision, actor role, and
# (for close) Coverage before/after fingerprints.
# `update_task.py` task-transition receipts additionally carry task id,
# completion_semantics,
# before/after task state, evidence receipt, actor/time, both Queue revisions,
# and before/after SHA-256 fingerprints for Coverage, Queue, and Progress.
# Their ordered task-state edges and timestamps are durable restart history,
# and the latest checkpoint binds the latest task transition. Legitimate Queue,
# replan, or Amendment transactions may change Progress bytes between two task
# transitions, so current checkpoint binding plus the intervening transaction
# receipts explains byte continuity; the task-transition subchain alone does not.
# `compile_queue.py` initial `queue_structure` receipts bind the unique origin
# of a materialized Queue: task, integrator role, Queue revision edge/state
# revision, and Coverage/Queue/Progress before/after fingerprints. Progress
# retains that receipt id in `initial_queue_receipt` across later changes.
# Same-scope `queue_replan` commit receipts additionally bind the repository-
# contained Coverage proposal path/SHA, exact affected pages/batches and diff
# SHA, transaction id/phase, the consumed registration_receipt, and all three
# canonical state before/after SHAs.
# A prepare receipt precedes the three-file replacement; an abort receipt
# records a handled failure. A crash or incomplete rollback retains the lock.
# `apply_amendment.py` transaction receipts additionally carry:
#   transaction_phase               : prepare / commit / abort
#   transaction_id / sequence       : append-only cross-Ledger transaction identity
#   previous_transaction_commit_receipt : prior committed transaction, or null
#   amendment_id / operation / plan_sha256
#   registration_receipt            : exact approved authorization consumed
#   before_*_sha256 / after_*_sha256 for Coverage, Queue, and Progress
# The prepare receipt and surviving writer-lock owner are recovery evidence if
# a process stops between per-file replacements; within the local trust
# boundary, a matching commit records successful transaction closure.
# `register_amendment.py` is the sole producer of executable operational
# Amendment rows. Its `amendment_registration` pass receipt binds the explicit
# approval reference, operation-specific plan/proposal/diff fields, current
# contract, and unchanged Coverage/Queue plus before/after Progress SHA-256.
# While the row is approved/pending, only the current filtered receipt catalog
# can authorize execution. After verified write-back the same receipt is
# resolved from immutable history and cannot authorize new work. The receipt is
# appended before its pending Progress row; an unreferenced receipt is inert.
# The consuming commit must bridge all three registration after-SHAs to its
# before-SHAs and may not predate registration.
# Canonical `apply_delta.py` receipts bind batch, exact delta SHA, Queue SHA,
# and Coverage before/after SHA. A worker delta receipt never authorizes
# Queue/compiler-owned control-field changes.
# Existing receipt IDs remain valid append-only protocol references after generator-format changes; never rewrite historical IDs.
# =============================================================================
